config SECURITY_UGOW
	bool "UGOW W-bit permission enforcement for WSL2 drives"
	depends on SECURITY
	depends on SECURITYFS
	default n
	help
	  Adds a per-path, per-UID "write bit" that gates write-class VFS
	  operations on filesystems registered for enforcement -- in practice
	  the Windows drives WSL2 mounts under /mnt.

	  Enforcement is opt-in per device: nothing is gated until userspace
	  registers a device, so enabling this option cannot by itself make a
	  filesystem read-only.

	  Grants are managed via securityfs at:
	    /sys/kernel/security/ugow/grant    (write "uid major:minor path")
	    /sys/kernel/security/ugow/revoke   (write "uid major:minor path")
	    /sys/kernel/security/ugow/devices  (write "+major:minor" / "-major:minor")
	    /sys/kernel/security/ugow/grants   (read to list grants)

	  Paths are superblock-relative; the userspace CLI strips the
	  mountpoint before writing them.

	  SECURITYFS is required: it is the only control interface, and
	  without it the module has no way to receive grants.

	  If unsure, say N.

config SECURITY_UGOW_ROOT_EXEMPT
	bool "Exempt root (uid 0) from UGOW W-bit checks"
	depends on SECURITY_UGOW
	default y
	help
	  When enabled, processes whose filesystem UID is 0 bypass W-bit
	  checks, matching the BPF backend's behaviour.

	  Say N only if you intend root itself to be gated. Be aware that
	  system services, package managers and container runtimes routinely
	  write to mounted Windows drives as root, and will start receiving
	  EACCES unless you grant uid 0 explicitly.

	  If unsure, say Y.
